## Choices (before code)
C01. **One module** labs/colab-support-pass01/colab_support.py, standard library only, with commands `preview`, `prepare`, `verify` and `intake`. It covers no review, find, scheduler or model call.
C02. **Request** `colab-request/1`: strict JSON (duplicate keys, BOM, trailing data, NaN/Infinity, wrong types and unknown keys refuse). It holds the exact `colab-binding/1` object as `binding`, plus title, date, author, recipient, reviewer, mode, scoring, context, question, why, built state, known gaps, method, cases, environment, permissions, stop, prior involvement, required sources, withheld, unavailable, Director replies and return route. Size and count limits are explicit.
C03. **Investigation origin** must be an N7NN member, 0701–0799, in the declared book. The door 0700 and any other band (N5NN and the rest) refuse. A target that names the investigation page itself refuses.
C04. **Target binding.** `source.path` must end in the segments `book/page/file`; nothing is guessed about the desk prefix. Each target's blob is read from Git at its commit and must match `source.sha256`; a mismatch is stale and refuses. A heading section is the exact line, outside fences, with an explicit 1-based occurrence when the line appears more than once; null occurrence with two matches refuses. The section runs to the next heading of the same or higher level. `kind: id` refuses because v1 declares no section-id syntax; that is a gap for Prime, not an invented convention.
C05. **Repository roots** come from a separate `colab-config/1` file (label to absolute root). They never appear in the package or the digest. The repository label does.
C06. **Assembler reuse, with an adapter.** The colab request is turned deterministically into a `pack-assembly-request/1`. The pinned assembler module is loaded in-process for preview (planning only, no writes), and the pinned assembler CLI is run by subprocess for the real assemble. Its raw stdout, stderr and exit are retained. Two limits follow from its shape and are refused, not worked around: every target and required source must share one repository and one commit, and recipient-facing assembler fields must not hold structural headings.
C07. **Package layout.** `OUT/package/` holds the assembler's exact files plus binding.json, ENTRY.md, 06.NN.md, RETURN-TEMPLATE.md and colab-request.json. Beside it, `OUT/prepared-colab.zip` is a deterministic ZIP of package/. `OUT/evaluator/` holds blinded evaluator material, outside the ZIP, only when a scored blind method is declared. `OUT/assembler-run.json` keeps the raw helper output. `OUT/verification.json` is written last.
C08. **Digest** `colab-package-digest/1`: length-prefixed parts covering the colab request bytes, canonical binding bytes, the assembler's own digest (request, commit, sources, assembler outputs), each colab-planned file's path, sha256 and length, and the planned ZIP. Preview is read-only. Prepare writes only after the recomputed digest equals the supplied one.
C09. **Collisions and replay.** A non-empty output is never overwritten. If it holds an intact package with the same digest, prepare reports `already-prepared` and writes nothing. A different or partial package refuses and stays on disk. There is no automatic retry or new name.
C10. **Verify** checks the exact package file set and hashes, recomputes the digest, re-resolves sections against the snapshots, checks the ZIP members against the package bytes, checks that evaluator files are absent from the candidate inputs, and runs the pinned assembler `verify` on a byte-identical projection of its own file set. That projection is needed because the extra colab files would fail its exact-set rule. Findings are tagged `pack-defect`, `input-defect` or `tool-limitation`; verify makes no design findings.
C11. **Intake** verifies the package, pins and runs the inspector, and requires the reservation origin to equal the binding's investigation book, page and pass. It then runs the pinned helper `capture`, then `verify`, then the inspector again, and keeps every raw output and exit. An identical replay is the helper's own `already-captured`; changed bytes are its conflict; a partial package stays as attention. It prints a small `colab-intake-provenance/1` object. It edits no work page and grants no review, acceptance or release.
C12. **Retention.** ENTRY, 06.NN and RETURN-TEMPLATE carry Hostile, Factfind, Overflow and Unprompted, plus Director replies verbatim with an `actual` or `hypothetical-fixture` label. Required omissions (`unavailable`) stay distinct from deliberate withholds. Tool limitations stay distinct from design findings. Unscored mode carries no score field. ZIP-only recipients get readable inputs, and absolute local paths in recipient-facing text refuse.
C13. **Tests** use disposable repositories, books and outputs, the real pinned assembler, checker, helper and inspector, and private in-process hooks only. Raw helper output and exits are retained.
C14. **Real use.** One real-source package targets 0411.colab/01.md `## Compute module — proposed operations` at c406b2a9. Its 0701 investigation binding is a declared FIXTURE, not a live investigation. No model is called. One labelled synthetic return goes through intake in a disposable book with origin 0701/05.01, and a wrong origin is refused.

## Risks to watch
R01. Re-implementing custody that the helpers already own. Mitigation: delegate, and keep raw outputs.
R02. Over-validating into a competing grammar. Mitigation: only the binding and request rules above.
R03. Claiming integration with Grok's adapter. I won't; REAL-USE gives the contract only.

